
=== MotherDuck

ifndef::pro[]

MotherDuck is only supported in the professional version of SymmetricDS.

endif::pro[]

ifdef::pro[]

Use `symadmin module install duckdb` to install driver files, or copy your own files into the `lib` sub-directory.

MotherDuck is a cloud service for DuckDB. SymmetricDS supports MotherDuck as a target platform where data can be loaded.
It connects with the DuckDB JDBC driver and loads data the same way as a <<DuckDB>> target.

==== Setup

MotherDuck is only supported as a write-only node in SymmetricDS. See <<Write-Only Node>> for details on setting up a write-only node in SymmetricDS.

Set up the node by using the <<Add Node,Connect Database>> wizard and selecting MotherDuck as the type. The JDBC URL names the MotherDuck database and
includes the access token:

----
jdbc:duckdb:md:<database>?motherduck_token=<token>
----

The database must already exist in MotherDuck. Unlike a local DuckDB file, a MotherDuck database is not created when SymmetricDS connects to it,
and the connection fails with the error "no database/share named ... found". Create the database in the MotherDuck UI or with a `CREATE DATABASE` statement.

Tables are created in the `main` schema of the database by default.

==== Access Token

SymmetricDS authenticates to MotherDuck with an access token. To create a token:

. Sign in to the MotherDuck UI.
. Open the organization menu, select *Settings*, and then *Access Tokens*.
. Create a token of type *Read/Write*.
. Copy the token.

The token is part of the JDBC URL and is saved in the engine properties file as entered, so restrict access to that file.

WARNING: If the URL has no token, the DuckDB driver opens a browser to sign in to MotherDuck. This is not compatible with SymmetricDS running as a
service, so always include the token in the URL.

==== Driver Version

MotherDuck accepts connections only from certain DuckDB versions. The DuckDB JDBC driver installed by the `duckdb` module must be a version that
MotherDuck supports. See the MotherDuck documentation for the supported versions.

==== Known Limitations
* MotherDuck is supported as a load-only (write-only) target. Triggers, change capture, initial load extraction, and log mining are not supported.
* Bulk loading is not supported. Data is loaded using JDBC statements.
* Tables, keys, and data types are created the same way as for a <<DuckDB>> target.

endif::pro[]
